course: dictionary
lesson: 6281
title: Monster and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Monster most often means a big, scary, made-up creature, as in «The monster in the film had huge teeth and glowing eyes.»
explain: But a monster can also be a very cruel or wicked person. It can mean something enormous, as in «a monster wave». When you meet a word, ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Look for clues around the word. The words «lake», «stories» and «scary» tell you a monster is a made-up creature. The words «truck» and «wave» tell you monster means very big. Context is your guide.
fact: A monster is a big, scary, made-up creature in stories, films and games.
fact: «Monster» can also mean a very cruel or wicked person.
fact: A «monster wave» or «monster truck» means something very big.
fact: «Little monster» is often a joke about a naughty child, not a real monster.
fact: Real monsters are not under the bed: if you are scared at night, tell an adult and use a night light.
exercise: «The monster in the film had huge teeth and glowing eyes.» What is a monster in stories? | A kind teacher who helps everyone | *A big, scary, made-up creature | A small pet that likes cuddles | A very slow train
exercise: Mum laughs, «You little monster, you ate all the cake!» What does she mean? | A giant wave | *A naughty child, said as a joke | A very cruel adult | A real creature from the lake
exercise: «The monster wave crashed over the harbour wall.» What does monster mean here? | *Something enormous | A made-up creature | A wicked person | A soft, gentle breeze
test: «There is no monster under the bed.» What should you do if you feel scared at night? | Go outside in the dark to look | Hide under the bed to find the monster | Stay still and say nothing to anyone | *Tell an adult and use a night light
test: Ana says, «A monster can be a very cruel person.» What is the best way to check her meaning? | *Put the meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Ask whether monsters are green | Look only at the pictures | Count the letters in monster
exam: «Yet the biggest animal of all time is not a lost monster but alive today: the blue whale.» What does this sentence tell you about monsters? | Monsters are heavier than any dinosaur | Monsters live in the deepest parts of the sea | *Monsters are made-up creatures from stories, not real animals | Monsters are the biggest animals alive today
